{"text": "In many situations, corporate moral decisions do not entail a trade off, doing the right thing is sometimes good for business. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Legal scholar Lucian Bebchuk calls this type of reasoning instrumental stakeholderism. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Making stakeholders customers, the community, employees, and so on happy, even if it costs a little, may be in the interest of shareholders. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> For Bebchuk, such situations do not pose a challenge to economist Milton Friedmans view that managers should aim at maximizing profits. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Giving an extra week of paid vacation or long maternity leave may help attract the most talented young college graduates. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Sourcing inputs responsibly may appeal to millennial consumers who are concerned with the environment. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Being more environmentally conscious than what current standards require might give a leading edge if one anticipates that regulations will become tighter in the near term. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Morality can be seen in such cases as an instrument for current and long term profits, not necessarily as a goal for its own sake.", "alignment": {"characters": ["I", "n", " ", "m", "a", "n", "y", " ", "s", "i", "t", "u", "a", "t", "i", "o", "n", "s", ",", " ", "c", "o", "r", "p", "o", "r", "a", "t", "e", " ", "m", "o", "r", "a", "l", " ", "d", "e", "c", "i", "s", "i", "o", "n", "s", " ", "d", "o", " ", "n", "o", "t", " ", "e", "n", "t", "a", "i", "l", " ", "a", " ", "t", "r", "a", "d", "e", " ", "o", "f", "f", ",", " ", "d", "o", "i", "n", "g", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "r", "i", "g", "h", "t", " ", "t", "h", "i", "n", "g", " ", "i", "s", " ", "s", "o", "m", "e", "t", "i", "m", "e", "s", " ", "g", "o", "o", "d", " ", "f", "o", "r", " ", "b", "u", "s", "i", "n", "e", "s", "s", ".", " ", "<", "b", "r", "e", "a", "k", " ", "t", "i", "m", "e", "=", "\"", "0", ".", "8", "s", "\"", " ", "/", ">", " ", "L", "e", "g", "a", "l",